This is not true. The UNSW also requires computer users to sign a
document in which the following appears:


	1. Resposible Use of Laboratories

         The workstation and PC laboratories are well-equiped and usually
	comfortable areas in which to work.... [...].. Be advised that the
	following are prohibited:

    * Smoking, eating or drinking in the labs. The equipment is far too
	susceptible to allow exceptions.
    * Attempting to interfere with the equipment in any way. Vandalism,
	theft or wilful damage will be referred to the police.
1-> * Tampering  with others' accounts in any way, including trying to thwart
	the system security, setting password traps, or other antisocial
	 behaviour.
2-> * Using the facilities for commercial purposes, or for preparing racist
	or other offensive material.
    * Using the equipment for recreational purposes when workstations
	are in demand by legitimate users.
    * Using other student's accounts in order to gain access privileges
	or booking tokens to which one is not entitled.
3-> * Not complying with a directive by the laboratory supervisors or
	other staff

If words are considered light and insignificant (as those on the network are) 
why should such a huff be caused by racist comments?  If those of tender
sensibilities know how to use a killfile, why should they care if
someone is posting (or emailing) antisocial words to them? Are they
afraid of finding out that somebody doesn't like them? If the art of
conversation were better known, messages can be parried with ease.
Coming down with laws and censoring attitudes does not promote the art
of conversation; it does, however, promote a culture of complaint and
silence.


Moreover, if the UNSW inquiry concludes that certain extra laws are required to
cover the electronic network (a kind of Racial Vilification Act, for
instance) and it contributes to such laws  being enacted, then the person who
signed the document and publishes the joke that Professor McCarthy
published in this newsgroup a week ago could face legal action; the
university would certainly have solid grounds to remove his access even
if no legal action was taken, as the student signed an undertaking, and
something he published was deemed racist by the authority.

I repeat that the ramifications extend beyond the concerns of the UNSW.


>It would
>be irresponsible and probably negligent of the university as an employer
>not to investigate what steps it can take, if any, to reduce racist and
>other harassment of its employees.

Indeed, if these comments are causing discomfort, then it ought to
investigate what can be done. I am arguing that in the case of offensive
comments disseimnated on email/internet, the best ways of reducing them
is through methods already existing; i.e. killfile, art of conversation,
pressing the "delete" button. In this way, others' freedom is not
cluttered up by unnecessary legislation.

Ideally, one would learn the art of conversation. The comment "I saw you
making out with the boss in the kitchen the other day Ha Ha!" is parried
by "Yeah, yeah, you couldn't even see if your girlfriend had the hots
for Fred" etc.   Suddenly snide comments lose their potency.

A worse option is to provide the means of defence.  The computer science 
department can provide unix software that makes it easy to kill files 
containing certain words or to send mail back automatically to particular 
senders.  



