wojdylo@maths.uwa.oz.au (INFIDEL) writes:
>It's obvious how they might think. I'm about to make a point for which
>my net access may be taken away:

I dont think this is even remotely likely. Whilst you are making a point
for which the university COULD CHOOSE to take away your access (if what
you say before is true) the likelyhood is extremely small. I think you
would have to be considerably more provocative to cause the VC to wake
out of his/her deeply comatose state and summon an underling to assist
with the royal finger-of-death.

>I want people on the net to be able to talk freely, to recognize the
>lightness of words.  At least.
              ^^^^^

This runs central to your argument. Since many of us have considerably
thinner skins, and actively wish to prevent abuse and hurt to ourselves
via the electronic media, surely we are intitled to expect the same
degree of protection afforded us when hurtful comment is made via
traditional means of expression? Do you deny any such protection
exists? 

Why must free speech rights override any other right such as privacy
and freedom from harrasment?

>Second, I never claimed a right to free net access, I claim a right to
>free expression. 

You can claim any right you like. As usual, society at large reserves the
right to disagree about the exact rights you can express at any given time.

I suspect your rights to free expression are more constrained than you
accept in general. I would prefer to see less constraint on expression
of ideas, and I too like the concept that 'words cannot hurt' but
reality is not so simple. Apart from anything else, your rights to free
speech are not formally stated under Australian law, Whilst the
constraints are detailed and many, state and federal.  This is a wider
issue demanding you address more fundamental rights of expression than
those specific to electronic media. No?

>I am quite prepared to pay for my net access (as it happens, I already do so 
>in kind).  

Hmmm. could you be more specific? Would the university or taxpayer see your
payment in the same light?

>If my net access is denied, I have already argued elsewhere why the authority 
>is obliged to provide an explanation that clarifies how it is consistent with 
>their notion of right to free speech which they profess to uphold 

Yes, I think you have succesfully established that anybody should demand
consistency across all forms of expression. Of course, special cases will
doubtless be argued quite successfully: The fact that no deployed method
of digital signature exists and the implications for forgery would rank high
in my mind as to why electronic communication is NOT completely synonymous
with speech and writing.

Of course, since currently access to the net is not axiomatic but subject
to application and consent by providers, Some of this is moot when the
issue is about 'when all have access' or 'when the net is truly free' - I'm
not very turned on by "pie in the sky" any more.

>(or, at least, they profess to uphold women's rights and other rights). 

Stop being silly. Rights aren't an all-or-nothing, you can quite easily
profess to uphold one set of rights and choose to deny another. Clearly
your university does so! Since you and they can't agree about what right
exists, how can you expect to agree which ones to profess?

>As things are now, most sysadmins don't even
>have a clear notion of what their role is, and many that I've heard of
>are easily swayed by the wrong arguments.  

I refute this. I have never yet met a system admin who took the role
of postmaster and news-flower lightly. When circumstance leads you to
read mail, the obligation to treat the material read as priviledged is
almost absolute dispite the clear legal precedent that the mail belongs
to the employer and not the sender or recipient.  I have much anguish
about my work, and certainly don't like simply enforcing my employers
wishes. Nonetheless my contract places me under their direction.  Yes,
the good old concentration camp guard defence plea. How tacky. 

What we don't take axiomatically is that news is yet (or will ever be)
a free speech communications method. I'd certainly like it to be, but
you're going to have to do a lot better to convince me.

	-The connecting media belongs to somebody else.
	-The connecting machines belong to somebody else.
	-Rights of access are not explicit and free of all encumberences.
	-Several federal laws already dictate constraints of use.
	-Parallel forms of one-to-many communication (television, radio,
	 newspapers) are subject to many constraints of use.

	Other forms of communication within the university are not
		unencumbered:

	-noticeboards are policed.
	-meetings are closed and subject to statute and rule.
	-campus itself is often private property.
	-telephones are subject to rules of use.
	-access to lecture theatres are subject to rules of use.

It doesn't look to me like your free speech rights exist in reality: why
are you fighting to defend them here instead of out there on campus where
they've already been stolen away?

>Third, network access is available to the public, through public
>companies. 10 years from now net access could be as popular as the home
>computer. Mistaken laws enacted now that limit people from talking freely
>will remain in effect for a long time. 

How are you going to be constrained from 'talking freely'? - I dont understand
what you feel unable to say, or expect to feel unable to say under forseen
constraint. How is this different to any other form of communication?

> Instead of a society where things
>are hushed up, I want one where people talk freely and understand the
>art of conversation.

It doesn't exist. It never has. If you want to champion a utopian ideal
by all means do so. By all means assert and defend your 'moral rights'
(and I will probably even agree, lukewarm, from a distance if you really
 want tepid milquetoast support) but please, don't expect lead to turn
into gold anytime soon.


