jchason@s.psych.uiuc.edu (Julie Chason) wrote:
>
>Of course men may discuss women's issues, but what I was referring to
>was the almost instant misogynistic response that trivialized the 
>issue at hand and implied (if not outright stated) that because this
>was something women were concerned with, it was somehow beneath 
>consideration.   

Don't disregard the lurkers.

If memory serves, the latest readership statistics estimated that the
alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk newsgroup had over 18000 readers.  Of that 
number, how many have posted in the last 30 days?  The last 6 months?  

In electronic media, it seems natural that those holding extreme 
points of view are far more likely to to post than those whose posi-
tions are closer to the "middle of the road."  The vast majority of
readers simply sit back watching (and, hopefully, considering) the
opinions as they fly across the net.

*Never* assume that those who post are an accurate reflection of
net consensus.  In one recent discussion, I received over 30 pieces
of email that basically said "well said -- I agree with you," but
*none* of those 30 people posted anything themselves.  [ For those
unfamiliar with my posts, I generally try to strike a "middle of
the road" position. ]

>And throughout the discussion of sexual harassment on uiuc.general,
>there have been a great many individual who presume to tell
>others how they *should* feel.

After reading the recommendations from the UIUC panel, I felt that
the *panel* was presuming to tell people how to feel; the absence of
any provisions for "talking it out" or "social recourse" implies that
the panel believes that filing judicial charges against presumed of-
fenders is (or should be) the sole recourse of the offended party. I 
also believe that, since the recommendations do not address intent, 
the panel would have us believe that *anything* an individual may 
find offensive, in *any* situation in a public lab, should be treated 
as harassment.

Would you agree that presumption goes both ways?

>And one great intellect even said that women should learn to act like
>men if they wanted *men's* jobs.   

Yeesh.  No excuse for some folks......

>WRT a different response--why haven't women been discussing this issue?
>I for one do not presume to speak for all women but here are some 
>hypotheses that may apply to some women.  And to some men, for that matter. 

You might want to add "expectation of failure" to that list.  Read
the article in this week's _Rolling Stone_ about the "water buffalo"
speech code incident at UPenn; the quotes of the woman advising the
ladies who filed the complaints were quite illuminating.  (I'm sorry
that I keep referring to this particular article; it is the one which
I most recently read, and I'm the most certain of its quotes.)  

Perhaps women, better than men, realize that harassment is an
individual issue; no policy or law can dictate what offends me, 
and no usage code can tell you when to be offended.

--Wes

