Beth Weiss writes:

>Are the male peers merely displaying images, or are they talking about
>that: "Hey look at the tits on this one?"  "wouldn't you like to jump
>her?" "I'd sure like to get her alone" [and on and more explicit and
>so on]

This again drives home the point that the _context_ of allegedly harassing
actions is crucial to a determination of whether or not they in fact constitute
harassment.

>[...] it is impossible for a woman who overhears them to know
>if they're talking about images on the screen or the woman in the
>room.
>
>If you add to the woman's situation the very real possibility that the
>building is empty other than for the men making comments about either
>her or the images on the screens, I would say that an unacceptably
>hostile environment exists--and she can't be expected to work there.
>If she has concern for her safety, she'll probably leave.  (I know I
>would have--as soon as I got a list of all people on-line and what
>they were doing so I could formally complain.)

All depends on how real the aforesaid "very real possibility" actually is.
Chris Burian's response to Weiss' post deems it unlikely, but it's a question
worth getting additional opinions on.

The potential "formal complaint" alluded to above, on the basis of a "list of
all people on-line and what they were doing," troubles me.  First, even if the
specific computing task being engaged in can be identified from an online list,
can exactly what gif is being viewed be pinpointed?  What if others, elsewhere,
are viewing gif's--"explicit" or otherwise?  Unless the location of a terminal
session can be pinpointed, an enumeration of active terminal sessions alone is
not conclusive evidence of anything.  What is more, the putative verbal harass-
ment is not integral to the computing task of viewing a gif, yet is a vital
contextual factor that needs to be considered.

>Even if they're not talking quite as explicitly, is it possible (even
>likely) that the only reason they've switched to sexual images is to
>make Barbara uncomfortable?  If so, then their behavior certainly does
>fall under the harrassment category.

_If_ that is in fact their motive, then I would agree.  But at face value, this
proposition sounds unduly conspiratorial to me.  It boils down, IMHO, to the
issue of how to determine accurately the presence or absence of a clear and
present danger.  Opinions, anyone?

>I think that universities have an obligation to provide safe,
>reasonable working places for their students.  If the environment
>encourages men to treat women as sexual objects, it just means they
>don't have to treat them seriously as academic peers.

All of which is quite true.  It does not automatically follow that vague and/or
overbroad definitions of sexual harassment, and procedures that facilitate
causes of action being brought on meager evidence, are acceptable solutions to
the problem.

