Actually, there are quite a few people who care about *every* viewpoint;
if we're going to work on global concepts, we *must* concern ourselves
with every viewpoint.

I'm all for strong policies against sexual harassment, just as I support
similar policies against racism, sexism, et al.  My dispute with the vast
majority of such policies, include those suggested by the UIUC panel, lies
in the determination of harassment itself.  Beth's suggestion that she'd
just "get a log of who was doing what" and the notion that such a log might
be considered valid evidence are examples (in my opinion) of the overly 
vague nature of most policies.  The same software that display nude GIFs
can also display the NASA satellite images, NWS weather maps, and images 
of documents from the Library of Congress; the mere fact that user XXX was 
running "xshowgif" doesn't prove squat, and any suggestion that everyone 
using such a program should be forced to provide evidence of 'valid use' 
(as would inevitably occur after submission of a log such as Beth suggested) 
is an unwarranted invasion of privacy.

Harassment should meet a 'standard of proof.'  The composition of that
standard is, in many ways, the most delicate step in the entire process.
In my opinion, the 'standard of proof' for harassment should cover:

	1) Direction of the alleged 'offensive material' to an 
	   individual or specific group.

	   I can't support the notion that "all women would find YYY
	   offensive"; it is, and should be, an individual decision.  
	   For instance, consider the famous photograph from the Mis-
	   sissippi civil rights conflict of the 1950s-1960s, in which 
	   a black man had been chained to a tree and tortured with a 
	   blowtorch.  I knew a student who used that picture as the 
	   background for his Xterminal.  Does that photo have historical 
	   significance? Certainly. Does it have academic significance?  
	   Most assuredly.  Is it offensive?  That decision can ONLY be 
	   made on an individual basis.  Incidentally, the student was 
	   black; does that make any difference at all?  *Should* it make 
	   a difference?  [ The picture can be found in "The Best of LIFE," 
	   a compendium of photographs from LIFE magazine.  Check it out. ]

	   To me, putting an image on *my* monitor should not be con-
	   strued as either "public" or "directed at others."  If I
	   force the display onto *your* monitor, that's another story;
	   if I project it onto a wall, that's a clear-cut broadcast.  If
	   I call you over to look at it, it may be harassment.  The
	   simple fact that I'm looking at something on my monitor should
	   not be construed as harassment of passers-by.

	   We've been discussing images; what about text?  I routinely
	   set up my Xterminal windows with large fonts, which are easily
	   visible from a distance of several feet.  If you look over my
	   shoulder and *read some text* which you find offensive, should
	   you file charges against me?  Can we, in fact, distinguish be-
	   tween images and text?  What about hypertext, which mingles
	   the two?

	   What if I warn potential viewers?  I once saw a student who
	   put a sign on his monitor that read "adult images -- don't
	   peek if you're easily offended."  Does that absolve the user
	   from responsibility for those who disregard the sign and look
	   anyway?  My monitor happens to face the interior of my office;
	   a visitor would have to walk all the way around behind my desk
	   to view it.  Should that make a difference?  What if someone
	   in a public lab selects a workstation in a corner and turns
	   its monitor such that "special effort" would be required to
	   view it; should someone who makes that "special effort" be
	   able to turn around and file charges?

	2) Intent to harass.

	   Harassment, by definition, is an intentional act.  If you
	   can't show intent, I don't think that you can call it haras-
	   sment.  Such intent could be demonstrated/illustrated by simply
	   asking the person to cease such activity.  The UIUC recom-
	   mendations make no provision for such actions, however; they
	   imply that the filing of charges is the sole recourse of the
	   offended party.  In the absence of some overriding need (such
	   as being required to view nude images for an Art class, or
	   centerfolds for the Women's Studies course on pornography;
	   both of these are logical situations), who among us would *not* 
	   cease offending activity upon the receipt of such a request?
	
	   To put it bluntly, almost everyone deserves a chance to rec-
	   tify a situation like this *without* resorting to the judicial
	   system.  Think about it; if someone refused a reasonable request,
	   a complaint against that person would be made even *stronger*
	   by their refusal.  Therefore, it's actually in the BEST interest 
	   of the offended party to make such a request.  Why not give them 
	   an opportunity to arrive at their own resolutions, instead of
	   pressing every situation into the judicial system?

	3) Offensive nature of the material.

	   This is the ultimate bugaboo.  Given the wide range of sensi-
	   tivities and opinions, how should we judge each individual
	   case?  For instance, I've seen CD-ROM images of medical data
	   that some folks would find offensive; the images illustrating
	   the symptoms of primary and secondary syphillis in males is
	   a particularly graphic example that comes to mind.  I believe
	   that we all know people to whom *any* nude image is offensive.
	   Where do we draw the line?  *Should* we try to draw lines in
	   such a personal area *at all*?

I don't have a solution; if I did, I would be working as a paid consul-
tant.  8)  However, overly broad (or overly vague -- they don't neces-
sarily go hand in hand) policies are NOT a solution.

--Wes

