George Michaelson (Ah, you are that George Michaelson and not an
Australian) asks


     Could Carl And John(s) collectively perhaps state what form
     the condemnation and curtailment of abuses should take on a
     network? Is there no action which can be taken? Is all they
     forsee a request to abstain from abuse but no encoded right
     to curtail it?

The use of net mail to abuse people should be subject to exactly
the same laws and legal practices as the use of previous methods
of communication.  The main offense is probably repeated abusive
email personally directed and sent to many recipients.  It should
be regarded as less harassing than repeated abusive phone calls
and perhaps more harassing than repeated letters.  Letters can
be wastebasketted without opening.  Email is a slightly more
difficult to avoid even seeing, whereas phone calls, especially
at odd hours are a substantial harassment even if the recipient
immediately hangs up.

Special administrative measures to deal with complaints should
be allowed only very rarely and then with quasi-judicial
procedures.

Someone emailed me privately about an event where a photographer who
had taken nude pictures of his girl friend and posted them after they
broke up.  On hearing a complaint from the girl friend's mother about
this, his employer promptly fired him.  This was all I heard about the
story.  Now I'll speculate.

Very likely, the employer had no procedure for hearings in such
matters.  However, imagine that the photographer offered the
following defenses.  (1) We had agreed in advance that I would
post the pictures as part of my activity as a photographer.
(2) She wasn't originally my girl friend but a paid model.
Later she decided that it would embarrass her with her mother
and reneged.

I have no reason to believe that these defenses (or others) were
possible, or even that the employer didn't offer a formal hearing.
My point is that the legal and administrative traditions that
apply in other matters, should apply whether computers are
involved or not.

As for email being a privilege.  I agree that eventually arrangements
should be made for individuals to pay for their own email, just as
when I write a personal letter on my office desk, I put my own
stamp on it.  However, today we who use our employers' computers
have acquired a kind of squatters' rights.  When we first started
putting terminals on everyone's desks in the Stanford AI Lab in
the early 1970s, there was a Stanford regulation that computers
only be used for work.  As Director of the Lab, I always ignored
this rule, taking the position that writing something personal
(or leftist political) on the computer was like writing it on
a Stanford desk.
