Subject: Democracy Bulletin Board? (on new network-to-be)
Message-ID: <1993Jul24.165640.15489@umiami.ir.miami.edu>
Date: 24 Jul 93 16:56:40 EDT

We are installing a computer network in our new building.  It will
be operational some time next calendar year.  Students will be
given accounts with at least local e-mail in the near term and
probably internet access in the longer term.

Yesterday I went to a faculty meeting to discuss the uses of the new
system, which will be running Netware 4.0.  

One of the ideas we were kicking around was to have a kind of
"Democracy Wall" area on the LAN in which students could post
things anonymously, either for the faculty/admin to read or for
everyone to read.  The idea would be to create a forum for students
to air any grievances they might have but be afraid to voice
openly, and also for them to let off steam.  There would be no need
whatsoever for any content regulation of this forum, since no one
would be forced to read the material unless they affirmatively
sought to do so.

I was wondering what readers of this group thought of the idea, and
particularly whether anyone has experience with such a "Democracy
Bulletin Board" (Whether this would provide any justification for time, place
and manner restrictions on other uses of the network is a separate
issue; my hope is that whether or not we implement this idea we
will have as few restrictions as possible).

   In addition to posting replies to the above, please e-mail me if you 
are aware of another group to which technical or policy issues dealing 
with setting up a new network in an academic environment should be addressed. I
have a feelling this is only my first question....

   
